  "answer": "Double circulation is a system in which blood passes through the heart twice during one complete cycle. In humans, this process is important because it ensures that oxygenated blood is efficiently delivered to all parts of the body while deoxygenated blood is returned to the lungs for re-oxygenation. The first circulation, called pulmonary circulation, involves the right ventricle sending blood to the lungs via arteries, where it picks up oxygen and returns via veins to the left atrium. The second circulation, called systemic circulation, involves the left ventricle sending oxygenated blood to the rest of the body via the aorta, and then returning deoxygenated blood to the right atrium. This double circulation allows for efficient oxygen and nutrient delivery to tissues and the removal of waste products, which is essential for maintaining life and supporting bodily functions.",
